.TH "cbor::lazy_elements" 3 "Version 0.9.0" "mbedded-cbor" \" -*- nroff -*-
.ad l
.nh
.SH NAME
cbor::lazy_elements \- The elements of one array, from \fBcbor::lazy::elements()\fP\&.  

.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.PP
\fR#include <cbor/lazy\&.hpp>\fP
.SS "Public Member Functions"

.in +1c
.ti -1c
.RI "iterator \fBbegin\fP () const"
.br
.ti -1c
.RI "std::default_sentinel_t \fBend\fP () const"
.br
.ti -1c
.RI "std::uint64_t \fBsize\fP () const"
.br
.ti -1c
.RI "void \fBfront\fP () const =delete"
.br
.in -1c
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP 
The elements of one array, from \fBcbor::lazy::elements()\fP\&. 

It is a forward range, a sized range and a borrowed range\&. \fRsize()\fP gives the count of the head\&. \fR*it\fP gives the lazy of the element, or the error of the element\&. An iterator holds the owner of the bytes, so it stays valid after the lazy and the view end\&. \fRfront()\fP does not compile\&. When the iterator cannot step over an element, \fR*it\fP gives \fRtoo_little_data\fP or another error of the bytes, and each later position gives that error\&. The view gives at most \fRsize()\fP values\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
message: [{"age": 31}, {"age": 20}, {"age": 40}]
call: auto const view = *doc\->elements()
result: lazy_elements of 3 elements: {"age": 31}, {"age": 20}, {"age": 40}
call: view\&.size()
result: 3
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
    // bytes: a std::string with [{"age": 31}, {"age": 20}, {"age": 40}]
    auto const people = cbor::lazy::adopt(std::move(bytes));
    auto const view = *people\->elements();  // 3 elements
    auto const older = std::ranges::count_if(view, [](std::expected<cbor::lazy, cbor::error> const &e) {
        return e && e\->at("age")\->get<int>()\&.value_or(0) > 30;
    });  // 2
.fi
.PP
 
.PP
